itionists  under the garrison ians and they were  anti-political they were called dis  unionists they actually wanted to  withdraw from the union and they were  anti political activity and most of them  became anarchists and because they had  the argument that they're arguing  against slavery is no person can rule  another and if they took that principle  and they applied it to government they  said well no person can rule another so  we're anarchist and and that's one camp  but there was this other camp that's not  known to be a camp of an ER of  abolitionists and one word that's used  to describe them is constitutional  abolitionists they believed in political  activity and they believed that the  Constitution actually was not  pro-slavery it was anti-slavery and they  pursued political action in order to end  slavery thief they founded initially the  Liberty Party which was a single-issue  anti-slavery party then they went on to  found the Free Soil party which was a  more moderate vision of this view and  eventually they formed the Republican  Party the constitutional plank of which  said that the Constitution does not  endorse slavery it just allows it in the  states that had it and  it was the constitutional commitments of  the Republicans that drove the South out  of the Union and ended slavery by means  of a war but let me just mention Spooner  here Spooner very interestingly was in  both camps he was an anti political guy  and but he kept his and so he wasn't a  fan of the liberty party in political  action and so he but he kept those views  quiet because he didn't want to alienate  his audience is he a lot of fans in that  clamp because he was a pro Constitution  guy so he was an in neither camp he was  neither he was against the garrison ians  anti constitutionalism he was against  the political activity of the  constitutionalist and as a result both  sides actually respected him quite a bit  especially the constitutional abolition  is he was kind of a he