e because they got in the  way if they didn't get in the way you  could just use them but you got to get  rid of them because they're in the way  you can't have it both ways you can't  say the Constitution we have is  consistent with all political or all  legislative ends  all laws and then get rid of the ones  that get in the way so the Constitution  is compatible with libertarian  principles it's compatible with them and  it it actually is what has kept us a  freer Society I mean the other way of  looking at the Constitution is that the  mean one of the reasons why Spooner  eventually turned away from the  Constitution which he did later on was  he said look the Constitution either  gave us the kind of tyrannical  government we have or it was incapable  of preventing it from taking place and  you know I'm very sympathetic with that  view I was completely sympathetic with  that few years ago I'm less sympathetic  I'm still sympathetic but I don't buy it  entirely because I think one of the  reasons why we have as free as society  as we have is because of what's left of  the Constitution here's a way of  thinking of it the founders built  constitution that was so well engineered  that it built in so and it built in so  many redundant checks on power that even  if you get rid of this one and this one  and this one in this one just the ones  that are left are enough to keep us  relatively free it's as though you're on  a four-engine jet you've lost three of  your engines the last remaining engine  is still keeping you aloft because the  jet was well designed it was designed to  fly on one engine although that's not  optimal and that's where we are now  we're flying on one engine the what's  left of the Constitution it's still good  enough to keep us aloft but it would  sure be a lot better if we could get all  the anti engines fired up well that's a  great place to end thank you very much  Randy Barnett professor at Georgetown  Law Center one of the legal architects  